          {
            "itemLocator": "confirmations[0]",
            "keyedAnswer": "[3, 4]",
            "kind": "twoDefensible",
            "workedAnswer": "The stem asks about the whole phrase, not specifically about the effect of personification. Index 3 captures the change from habitual noise to post-funeral stillness; index 4 explains why that stillness feels like a gesture of respect. Both answer the singular question."
          },
          {
            "itemLocator": "confirmations[1]",
            "keyedAnswer": "[1, 4]",
            "kind": "wrongKey",
            "workedAnswer": "Only index 4 explains the concession's argumentative role: the critic acknowledges that some images fail, making the final praise more credible. Commenting on the middle section does not establish that the critic read every poem, as index 1 claims."
          },
          {
            "itemLocator": "confirmations[2]",
            "keyedAnswer": "[3, 4]",
            "kind": "noCorrectOption",
            "workedAnswer": "The question requires a premise the moderator does not state directly. Yet the moderator explicitly says the hours were treated as fixed, that nobody questioned protecting them (index 3), and that the city was expected to protect them once the numbers worked out (index 4). What the closing request implies is that the committee should examine its priorities rather than treat funding as the only issue; no option gives that inference."
          },
          {
            "itemLocator": "confirmations[3]",
            "keyedAnswer": "[3, 4]",
            "kind": "twoDefensible",
            "workedAnswer": "Index 4 captures the unresolved choice in the final question. Index 3 also has textual support: coming just after the aunt's account of Grandpa crying, “just a road trip?” gently undercuts his claim of success. The question can express irony without settling how the family should tell the story."
          }
        ],
